From 49e2012f6c848ee173b639385e6ddd255e7ef081 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 20:13:25 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] chat: report progress for a running tool call

A host has no way to say that a running tool call is still working, so a
long call is indistinguishable from a stalled session. Every existing
mechanism fails on a specific ground: `chat/toolCallContentChanged` is
the only action valid on a running call but replaces its content
wholesale, so a liveness report would have to fabricate or clobber tool
output; `chat/activityChanged` is chat-scoped and carries no tool call
id; `root/progress` is root-channel-only, obtainable only from a
`createSession` token, and is not replayed on reconnect;
`chat/toolCallDelta` no-ops unless the call is still streaming its
parameters.

`chat/toolCallProgress` carries the elapsed time on the producer's own
clock plus an optional description of the current step, reducing onto
`ToolCallRunningState.progress`. It is state rather than an ephemeral
notification so a client that reconnects mid-call can still render that
work is ongoing rather than silence.

Each report replaces the previous one wholesale, and progress is dropped
when the call leaves `running`: a completed call states its own duration,
so stale elapsed time could only contradict it. `elapsedMs` is
milliseconds, matching every other duration in the protocol, which also
keeps sub-second reporting representable without a float.

Reducer parity across the Go, Kotlin, Rust and Swift clients, with five
shared conformance fixtures covering the running update, a bare liveness
report, wholesale replacement, and the two no-op paths.
